Traditional SNNs derive their computational efficiency predominantly from presynaptic input-driven sparsity.
A synaptic operation is executed strictly when an active input spike is emitted from the preceding layer n − 1 n-1 .
Thus, the total SOPs for a baseline spiking layer n n equate to its dense MACs scaled by the average temporal firing rate of the input spikes, denoted as R n − 1 t ¯ \overline{R_{n-1}^{t}} : SOPs SNN , n = MACs n ⋅ ∑ t = 1 T R n − 1 t ¯ \text{SOPs}_{\text{SNN},n}=\text{MACs}_{n}\cdot\sum_{t=1}^{T}\overline{R_{n-1}^{t}}

